  "query_preview": "..\nSunday through Wednesday afternoon: As the low moves further \nsoutheastward, clouds will continue to clear from the Inland \nnorthwest moving eastward as well. The last of any residual fog is \nfinally dissipating. By later this evening, most areas apart from \nShoshone County in Idaho will see clear skies. This clearing, \nalongside a moist boundary layer and light winds, leads to the \npotential for fog formation tomorrow morning. Areas that look to see \nthe best potential for fog are the Spokane area down into the \nColumbia Basin, which see RHs closest to or at 100%. Should dense \nfog form as it did this past Saturday morning, make sure you slow \ndown and use your low beams if you encounter fog tomorrow morning. \nAside from light showers in the Cascades on Tuesday as a low passes \nto our south, things look relatively quiet through Wednesday \nafternoon. Highs will continue to be near normal (high 40s to low \n50s), as will lows (low to mid 30s). \n\nWednesday night through Friday: Wednesday night into Thursday, the \nfirst of two mid to end of week systems moves through. A trough will \nmove through, bringing a chance for light rain and mountain snow. \nSnow levels with this system will be very high, 5000 feet and above, \nand totals will be low, with Stevens Pass expected to see an inch or \nless and Washington Pass expected to see 2-3 inches. From Moses Lake \nto Omak and Southward, expect light precipitation with totals under \n0.10 inches. \n\nThursday night into Friday, the next system will move in, bringing \nmore rain and mountain snow to the area. Stevens Pass has an 11% \nchance of seeing 4 or more inches of snow Thursday night through \nFriday night, and Washington Pass has a 26% chance. Snow levels will \nbe slightly lower with this storm, around 4500 feet. The rest of the \nforecast area looks to see rain, but not much, with most of the area \nseeing 0.10 inches or lower. The highest totals will be seen in the \nCascades and ID Panhandle mountains, where there is a 30 percent \nchance of a quarter-inch of rain. As this system moves in, a \ntightening pressure gradient will lead to breezy winds in the \nOkanogan Valley Friday afternoon and evening, though impacts look to \nbe minimal. \n\nSaturday through Sunday: Ridging over the PNW will take place as a \nstrong low stays put off the Alaskan and Canadian coast. 70 percent \nof the ensembles show higher heights over the entire forecast area, \nand temperatures will rise a few degrees by Saturday and Sunday. \nSmall shortwaves along the ridge will promote continued showers, \nespecially in the Cascades. Confidence decreases into next week, \nwith clusters torn on the strong low impacting the PNW or staying \noff the coast with the ridge prevailing. /AS",
